# Study Gacha

A website that ties real study effort to gacha rewards. The user logs study time in a project folder, takes a short AI-generated quiz on their material, and earns pull shards based on time studied and quiz score. Shards are spent pulling pixel-art collectibles.

Core loop: Study (timer) -> Quiz (score) -> Reward (shards -> pulls).

## Stack

- Next.js (App Router) + TypeScript (strict mode)
- Tailwind CSS, with the theme restricted to the design tokens below
- Zustand for client state (timer, inventory UI) only where React state is not enough
- Prisma + SQLite for persistence, introduced at Milestone 2. Single local user until auth arrives in Milestone 11.
- Anthropic Claude API, called only from Next.js route handlers (server side). The API key lives in `.env.local` and must never reach the client bundle.
- Vitest for tests
- Layout is desktop-first but must work at phone width. A PWA or app wrapper can come later; do not build for it now.

## How to work

- Do one milestone at a time. Build only what the current prompt asks for.
- No extra features, no speculative abstractions, no "while I'm here" refactors.
- Pure logic (shard formula, weighted pulls, quiz JSON validation) lives in plain TypeScript modules with no React or Next imports, and gets unit tests.
- Game data (item list, rarity weights, formula constants) lives in data/config files, never hardcoded inside logic.
- Before finishing a task, run lint, typecheck and tests, and fix failures.
- Keep files small and named by purpose. If unsure where something goes, put it next to the feature that uses it.
- If a requirement is ambiguous, ask instead of guessing.
- The guardrails test (`theme/guardrails.test.ts`) must pass. Never bypass it.
- Always read the SQL of any generated migration before applying it. Prisma does not know about `StudySession_one_running`; never drop it, and keep `prisma/migrations.test.ts` passing.

## Design constraints (non-negotiable)

- **Typography:** system fonts only. Use a system font stack (`system-ui, -apple-system, "Segoe UI", Roboto, sans-serif`), which renders San Francisco on Apple devices and Roboto on Android. No web fonts, no font downloads.
- **Palette:** flat, solid background colors. Max 3 colors total for the UI. Absolute ban on gradients of any kind.
- **Layout:** generous whitespace, stark grid lines, high structural contrast. Do not fill the UI with explanatory text boxes.
- **Component philosophy:** early Apple, Notion, premium industrial design. Not a futuristic sci-fi dashboard.

Interpretation:

- The 3-color limit applies to the UI chrome (backgrounds, text, lines, buttons). Collectible artwork is the only exception and may use its own palette.
- Rarity is shown with border weight (hairline, medium, heavy), pips (small flat squares in the corner) and the color of the item text. The item-text colors come from `rarityPalette` in `theme/tokens.ts` (Terraria's tiers 0 to 11, flat hex, starting values to tune) and apply only to item names, rarity words and pips. They are not UI colors, like collectible artwork. **Rarity colors are used for item names, rarity words, pips, and the Last pull panel border only. Nothing else.** Item text is bold with a dark outline (`.item-text` in `app/globals.css`), drawn by `components/ItemName.tsx`, whose `size` prop (`small` for caption-size text, `large` for the Last pull name) picks the outline width and letter spacing from `itemTextOutline` in `theme/tokens.ts`. Pips use the same color with a hairline ink outline. The Last pull panel is always the normal light panel; rarities with `heavyBorder` (Super Rare, Ultra Rare) get a heavy (3px) border in their rarity color inside the normal ink hairline border. The border is static: any flashing belongs to the pull scene. Each rarity in `data/gacha.ts` has a `tier`, and `validateGachaData` throws if a tier is missing from the palette. Do not add custom (gradient) tiers 12 to 15.
- Item text uses `fontFamily.item`, which is the system stack today. It is an exception to "system fonts only" only if and when the user chooses a font. Do not add a font file or web font on your own.
- Tailwind's default palette is disabled. The theme defines only the 3 token colors, so off-palette classes simply do not exist.
- All colors, spacing, and type sizes come from the theme. No inline hex values, arbitrary Tailwind values (`[#fff]`, `[13px]`) or magic numbers in components.
- No box-shadow, glow or gradient effects. Blur is allowed only on the modal backdrop (`components/Modal.tsx`, the `backdropBlur` token). No other blur, glow or shadow; the guardrails test fails if a blur class appears anywhere else. Animations are flat and simple (scale, fade, translate), and the modal has none.

## Pixel art

- Sprites and the room layout are data in `data/sprites.ts` (rows of characters, `.` transparent, a small palette per sprite). Pure logic is in `lib/sprite.ts`; components are `PixelSprite` and `RoomScene`.
- One tile is 16px. The room is 10 by 6 tiles (160 by 96 logical pixels).
- Scale is a whole number only: 2x below `md` (phone), 4x from `md` up, through the `roomSize` tokens in `theme/tokens.ts`. Never a fractional scale.
- Render with SVG and `shape-rendering="crispEdges"`. No canvas, no image files.
- Sprite and room palettes are exempt from the 3-color rule, like collectible art. Hex values only in `data/sprites.ts`.
- Art is placeholder until Milestone 11. Do not generate or embed binary art assets unless the prompt says to.
- No animation beyond sprite frames (2 frames per pose, 500 ms each). Sprite frames stop under `prefers-reduced-motion` and while the page is hidden. The room shows only whether a timer is running; it reads no clock.

## Economy reference (from the design doc)

- Shards earned = (minutes studied / 10) x success rating (0 to 1)
- Implementation (`lib/economy.ts`, `calculateShards`): shards are whole numbers, floored per session, computed with integer arithmetic from `correctAnswers / totalQuestions` (counts, not a float rating). One session counts at most `maxCountedSecondsPerSession`. Constants live in `data/economy.ts`. Invalid input throws a `RangeError`.
- Rarity pull rates: Common 70%, Rare 22%, Super Rare 6%, Ultra Rare 2%
- Minimum session: 600 seconds (`minSessionSeconds`). A shorter session cannot unlock a quiz; exactly 600 is allowed (`sessionEligibility`).
- Material cap: pasted material supports 2 seconds of study per character (`secondsPerSourceChar`), so 300 characters support 10 minutes. Counted time is the smallest of the session length, the per-session cap (4 hours) and the material cap (`calculateAward`).
- Daily cap: 30 shards (`dailyShardCap`) from study sessions over a rolling 24 hours (`dailyWindowSeconds`), counted from ledger rows that have a `sessionId`. Not a calendar day.
- One quiz per session, enforced by a unique index on `Quiz.sessionId`. A failed generation does not use up the quiz. A second payout for a session is blocked by a unique index on `ShardLedger.sessionId`.
- Shards are awarded only inside the grading transaction (`lib/quiz/grading.ts` with `lib/rewards.ts`): claim the quiz, compute the award from stored data, append the ledger row, all or nothing. The ledger is append-only.
- Dev tools (the +shards grant, the add-minutes buttons) are development-only. They do nothing and render nothing when `NODE_ENV === "production"`, validate on the server, and never edit or delete ledger rows. No tool resets the daily cap; change `dailyShardCap` in `data/economy.ts` to retest it.
- Never send a duration, score or amount from the client. The server computes all of them from stored data.
- These numbers are starting values and must be easy to change in config.

## Timer rule

The timer stores a start timestamp and computes elapsed time from it. Never count by incrementing a counter in an interval, so a refresh, tab sleep or closed laptop does not lose or inflate time.

## Quiz

- Never send a duration, score or amount from the client.
- Quizzes must not be beatable by option length. The model is asked for `candidateQuestions` (10) questions, ordered best first, and `selectBalanced` keeps the `questionCount` (5) most balanced. A kept question passes the length rule by characters and by words when the longest option is within `maxOptionLengthRatio` (2.0) of the shortest, or within `maxOptionCharGap` (30) characters or `maxOptionWordGap` (3) words of it (options up to `shortOptionExemptChars` are exempt). At most `maxLengthTellQuestions` (1) kept question may have a unique-longest correct answer, and at most 1 a unique-shortest one. A rejected attempt is retried up to `maxAttempts` (3) times, with trusted numbers-only feedback. Do not weaken these without asking.
- Stopping a session of `quizPromptMinSeconds` (1800) or more opens the "Test yourself now?" dialog. The time, the most shards and the material needed are all computed on the server (`stopTimerAction`); the client sends nothing.

